---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { APPS, SEGMENTS, USE_CASES, description, pageTitle, siteFromHost } from '../content';
const site = siteFromHost();
const path = '';
const title = pageTitle(site, path);
const desc = description(site, path);
const recommended = APPS.filter(a => a.channels.includes(site.channel) || a.channels.includes('marketing'));
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path="/">
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden border-b border-cyan-300/30 py-16 md:py-24">
    <div class="container grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[1.05fr_.95fr] lg:items-center">
      <div>
        <span class="badge">Shopify SMS buyer guide</span>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-6">Choose Shopify SMS apps around the workflow you need.</h1>
        <p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl text-xl text-cyan-100">Compare consent records, Shopify events, message costs and channel ownership before choosing a sender. Start with one customer moment, then use the catalog and playbooks to identify the requirements your team can demonstrate.</p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-4"><a class="btn btn-primary" href="/apps">Open app radar</a><a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/compare">Scan comparisons</a></div>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <div class="text-sm font-black uppercase tracking-[.22em] text-cyan-200">Start with the operating model</div>
        <h2 class="mt-4 text-3xl font-black text-white">Decide who owns SMS and how it coordinates with email.</h2>
        <p class="mt-5 text-cyan-100">Ask each supplier to demonstrate permission history, queued-send cancellation, recipient timing controls and purchase suppression for your store. Use the same scenario across candidates.</p>
        <ul class="mt-6 space-y-3 text-sm text-cyan-100"><li>▣ Use SMS for urgent moments, not filler newsletters.</li><li>▣ Score tools by consent, segmentation, cost control, and flow clarity.</li><li>▣ Pair SMS with email when the message needs space, proof, or education.</li></ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container py-14">
    <div class="mb-8 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-[1fr_auto] md:items-end"><div><span class="badge">Catalog discovery</span><h2 class="mt-3 text-3xl font-black text-white">Explore apps and their evaluation notes</h2><p class="mt-3 max-w-3xl text-cyan-100">Catalog order is navigation, not a performance ranking. Open an app page to examine its role, then compare the capabilities needed for your workflow.</p></div><a class="link" href="/apps">View all apps →</a></div>
    <div class="grid gap-5 md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">{recommended.map((app) => <a class="card" href={`/apps/${app.slug}`}><div class="text-sm font-black text-cyan-200">App profile</div><h3 class="mt-3 text-xl font-black text-white">{app.name}</h3><p class="mt-3 text-sm text-cyan-100">Review workflow fit, channel scope and integration requirements.</p><p class="mt-5 text-sm font-black text-cyan-50">Open evaluation notes →</p></a>)}</div>
  </section>
  <section class="border-y border-cyan-300/20 bg-slate-950/60 py-14"><div class="container grid gap-8 md:grid-cols-3"><div><span class="badge">Page network</span><h2 class="mt-3 text-3xl font-black text-white">Find a workflow before choosing a tool</h2><p class="mt-4 text-cyan-100">Cart recovery, restock alerts and replenishment require different event data and cancellation rules. Start with the playbook for the customer moment you intend to support.</p></div><div class="grid gap-4 md:col-span-2 sm:grid-cols-2">{USE_CASES.slice(0,8).map(u=><a class="card" href={`/use-cases/${u.slug}`}><h3 class="font-black text-white">{u.name}</h3><p class="mt-2 text-sm text-cyan-100">Use SMS to {u.job}, with consent and margin checks built into the plan.</p></a>)}</div></div></section>
  <section class="container py-14"><h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Store-type playbooks</h2><p class="mt-3 max-w-3xl text-cyan-100">Different Shopify categories deserve different SMS behavior. A supplement refill reminder, a fashion drop alert, and a wholesale reorder nudge should never feel like the same campaign with swapped nouns.</p><div class="mt-6 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-3">{SEGMENTS.slice(0,9).map(s=><a class="card" href={`/for/${s.slug}`}><h3 class="font-black text-white">{s.name}</h3><p class="mt-2 text-sm text-cyan-100">Built around {s.pain}.</p></a>)}</div></section>
  <section class="container pb-16"><article class="prose-shopify card"><h2>How to read the SMS app radar</h2><p>This homepage is built for Shopify operators who already know SMS can recover revenue, but also know the channel can be damaged quickly. Use the app radar by starting with the kind of interruption your store can justify. Checkout recovery, VIP launch access, replenishment, back-in-stock alerts, and urgent account prompts are very different from generic promotional blasts. The right SMS app should make those distinctions operational before the first campaign is sent.</p><p>Begin with consent and cost. A platform should show where each subscriber opted in, what they expected to receive, how quiet hours are handled, and which customers should be suppressed from a send. It should also make message economics visible. SMS revenue must be judged after carrier fees, message fees, discounts, unsubscribes, and the long-term effect on customer trust. A tool that encourages broad sends may look exciting in a dashboard while quietly reducing the value of the list.</p><p>Then review Shopify context. A good SMS app should know products viewed, carts started, checkout state, purchase history, discount use, fulfillment timing, and repeat purchase windows. That data decides whether a text is relevant enough to interrupt someone. A replenishment reminder that lands at the right time feels helpful. A generic discount two days after purchase feels careless. The same app can create either outcome depending on how clearly it exposes triggers and exclusions.</p><p>Use the internal pages to narrow the decision. App reviews explain vendor fit. Comparison pages force tradeoffs. Store-type pages adapt SMS to categories like fashion, supplements, food, pets, and wholesale. Use-case pages translate abstract features into specific flows. The goal is not to send more texts. The goal is to protect a high-attention channel so customers still trust it after busy launch seasons, holiday promotions, and retention experiments.</p></article></section>
  <section class="container pb-16">
    <div class="grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-[1.05fr_.95fr]">
      <article class="prose-shopify card">
        <h2>SMS implementation depth: what should happen before the first text</h2>
        <p>A Shopify SMS program should begin with a channel permission audit, not a campaign calendar. Confirm every opt-in source, disclosure, keyword, checkout checkbox, popup promise, and suppression rule. Then map the customer moments where immediacy is actually helpful: cart or checkout recovery, limited inventory, replenishment, back-in-stock, VIP access, shipping-sensitive reminders, and urgent account prompts. If the message requires paragraphs of proof, send email and let SMS point to it only when timing matters.</p>
        <p>A proposed staged rollout begins by verifying consent, quiet hours, branded links, reply handling, and Shopify event reliability. Next, launch one high-intent flow with strict exclusions. After reviewing that flow, consider either a post-purchase reassurance text or replenishment path for products with a real repeat window. Then review cost per delivered message, margin after discount, opt-outs, replies, and whether SMS changed repeat purchase behavior rather than merely claiming last-click revenue.</p>
        <h3>Buyer evaluation criteria</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Consent and compliance:</strong> opt-in proof, quiet hours, STOP handling, disclosure visibility, and campaign-level suppression.</li>
          <li><strong>Shopify context:</strong> checkout state, product interest, order count, fulfillment status, discount history, inventory, and replenishment timing.</li>
          <li><strong>Interruption quality:</strong> whether the app encourages fewer, sharper texts instead of broad promotional blasts.</li>
          <li><strong>Economics:</strong> message fees, carrier fees, coupon cost, unsubscribe pressure, and downstream repeat purchase quality.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <div class="grid gap-4">
        <div class="card"><h3 class="text-2xl font-black text-white">Example: abandoned checkout</h3><p class="mt-3 text-cyan-100">Send only after meaningful checkout intent, suppress recent buyers and open support cases, reference the cart without sounding creepy, and avoid the first-message coupon unless product margin and customer stage justify it.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><h3 class="text-2xl font-black text-white">Example: replenishment</h3><p class="mt-3 text-cyan-100">Use SKU-specific usage windows, subscription state, last purchase date, and inventory status. A reminder for a thirty-day supplement is different from a seasonal flavor or a pet product sized by animal weight.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><h3 class="text-2xl font-black text-white">Example: product launch</h3><p class="mt-3 text-cyan-100">Segment VIPs, waitlist subscribers, recent purchasers, and discount-only shoppers separately. SMS should protect access and timing, while email carries details, proof, and merchandising depth.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container pb-16"><article class="prose-shopify card">
<h2>Choose a stack by the constraint you need to solve</h2>
<p>An SMS specialist and a combined email/SMS platform create different ownership costs. A specialist may let the SMS operator work independently, but the team must connect consent, purchase suppression and reporting across systems. A combined platform reduces the number of interfaces; it still needs explicit rules for which channel gets priority. Neither architecture proves better revenue.</p>
<p>Bring three examples to a vendor demonstration: a checkout that completes while a reminder waits, a restocked variant with more subscribers than units, and a subscriber who withdraws permission while another campaign is queued. Ask the vendor to show the resulting state and exportable record, rather than only the message editor. These are proposed evaluation cases, not tests conducted by this directory.</p>
<h3>Build a comparable cost request</h3>
<p>Give each supplier the same destination countries, SMS/MMS mix, expected segments, subscriber count and seasonal peak. Request platform minimums, carrier charges, registration charges, overages and support terms separately. Include the cost of keeping a second email system and the operator time needed to reconcile suppression. Do not compare a headline subscription fee with another vendor's complete messaging bill.</p>
<h3>Plan the exit before the installation</h3>
<p>Ask what can be exported: permission source and wording, timestamps, opt-outs, conversation history, event identifiers and flow definitions. Name the owner of the sending number and ask how migration affects it. Keep historical suppressions available during the move; a contact export alone is not a migration plan.</p>
<h3>Use evidence at the right scope</h3>
<p>Shopify's <a href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/reports-and-analytics/shopify-reports/report-types/default-reports/marketing-reports">marketing report documentation</a> explains how attribution models distribute credit. A credited order is not proof that SMS caused an additional purchase. Use the <a href="/use-cases/analytics">analytics playbook</a> to design a measurement plan before adding channels.</p>
<p>App pages are discovery routes, not a scored benchmark or a promise that every listed tool sends SMS. Confirm channel availability, Shopify integration, destination support and the required plan with the supplier. Sequenzy remains in the catalog for evaluation alongside other lifecycle tools; inclusion does not establish a native SMS capability or a validated integration with another sender.</p>
</article></section>
</BaseLayout>
